<Base title="About · LENS" active="about">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="eyebrow">About this site</div>
    <h1>About Capability Matters</h1>

    <p>
      Capability Matters connects learning engineering to what people need to do in
      complex systems. It supports Johns Hopkins’ Learning Engineering for
      Next-Generation Systems (LENS) specialization through case studies,
      interactive experiments, and prototype design work.
    </p>
    <h2>Find what you need</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/case-studies/">Case Studies</a>: documented examples, sources, and limits on interpretation. Includes the draft casebook.</li>
      <li><a href="/experiments/">Experiments</a>: learning labs, research projects, and creative demonstrations you can explore.</li>
      <li><a href="/problems-to-prototypes/">Prototypes</a>: capability gaps, design briefs, available tools, and plans to evaluate them.</li>
      <li><a href="/show-your-work/">Show Your Work</a>: a LENS community paper on capability, coupled learner and system performance, and human flourishing.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For the wider field’s reading lists, tools, events, and community, visit the
      <a href="https://lecommons.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Learning Engineering Commons</a>
      and <a href="https://sagroups.ieee.org/icicle/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEEE ICICLE</a>.</p>

    <h2>Where it applies</h2>
    <p>
      LENS focuses on high-consequence, high-impact domains — where human capability is
      inseparable from system performance and the cost of getting it wrong is measured in
      lives, readiness, and equity. Seven recur across the program's case material and
      teaching: healthcare, defense &amp; national security, education at scale, aviation,
      energy, manufacturing &amp; industrial, and government &amp; public infrastructure.
    </p>

    <h2>The LENS framework</h2>
    <p>
      LENS reads every case through five competency domains. Each case study on this site is
      tagged to the domain(s) it exercises, so the same framework runs from a hospital to a
      flight deck to a public-sector IT migration:
    </p>
    <ol class="framework">
      <li><strong>Systems Analysis</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Iterative Development</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Human-System Collaboration</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Test &amp; Evaluation</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Sociotechnical Constraints</strong></li>
    </ol>

    <h2>People &amp; programs</h2>
    <p>
      LENS is a concentration within the <a href="https://education.jhu.edu/masters-programs/master-of-education-in-learning-design-and-technology/learning-engineering-next-generation-systems-concentration/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JHU MEd in Learning Design and Technology</a>,
      jointly run by the Johns Hopkins School of Education and the Applied Physics Laboratory,
      with an IEEE ICICLE partnership. Faculty in the concentration include James Diamond and
      William Gray-Roncal (School of Education / APL). Full profiles for practitioners across the
      wider field live at <a href="https://lecommons.org/community/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">lecommons's community pages</a>.
    </p>

    <p>For Will Gray-Roncal’s other writing and projects, visit <a href="https://will.grayroncal.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">his personal site</a>.</p>

    <h2>Scope &amp; caveats</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>LENS-focused, not comprehensive — on purpose.</strong> This site stays narrow;
        it doesn't try to be a complete index of the field. For that, see lecommons.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>ICICLE is authoritative.</strong> When ICICLE and this site differ on a
        definition or taxonomy, defer to ICICLE. This site doesn't claim to speak for IEEE
        ICICLE or host its content — it connects to it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Links may rot.</strong> External resources change. File an issue if you find one.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Tools used</h2>
    <p>
      Claude (Anthropic) was used as an assistant throughout development and content
      curation — for code generation, draft copy, and triage of the underlying material.
      Final editorial decisions, scope, and accuracy review are human-authored.
    </p>

    <h2>Contributing</h2>
    <p>
      Suggestions and corrections are welcome. Open an issue or PR in the repository.
    </p>
  </div>
